Program the NPU SRAM read margin with the rail voltage

NPU_GRF_MEM_CON0/1/2 carry a memory read-margin field that the vendor
derives from the operating voltage and rewrites on every transition. The
port dropped it along with the rest of the vendor OPP framework, so it
has been sitting at its reset value of 4 while the rail moves between
750 and 850 mV, where the vendor asks for 3 and 2.

That is not a conservative default. Read-margin settings trade voltage
tolerance against SRAM access timing, so the reset value being the
strongest at low voltage says nothing about behaviour at 800 and 900 MHz,
and it is not something throughput testing can settle - it takes weak
silicon, temperature and particular data patterns to show up.

The ordering is the substance of it, so this is the OPP core's regulator
step rather than something run after dev_pm_opp_set_rate(), which would
already have moved both voltage and clock. The margin is programmed while
the rail is at the higher of the two voltages - after it rises when
scaling up, before it falls when scaling down - and put back if the
regulator call then fails.

The regulator stays the driver's own and is not handed to the OPP core:
_opp_set_regulators() overwrites any custom config_regulators with its
own single-regulator helper, so registering one through regulator_names
uninstalls the callback and none of this would run.

The suspend park needs handling of its own. It drops the clock with a
bare clk_set_rate() the OPP core never sees, so its cached rate survives
and the restore on the way back can be a no-op that never reaches the
callback - while the hardware has lost the margin across the power cycle.
The park invalidates the cached value and the restore programs the margin
explicitly before raising the clock.

Failed register writes are reported rather than cached as done, since a
partial write leaves the three banks disagreeing, and a present but
unusable device tree configuration fails probe instead of silently
dropping the setting.

The failure paths matter as much as the ordering. A caller holding a
runtime-PM reference can still find hw_powered false, because
rknpu_power_off() clears the gate before it drops the reference, so that
case answers -EBUSY rather than reporting a write it did not do and
letting the transition continue to a new OPP with a stale margin. A
missing OPP for the frequency being restored is an error too, since
returning success there would let the clock rise unprogrammed.

The cached value is invalidated at the start of every physical power-on
rather than only in the park. Hardware comes back at the reset margin
whatever happened, and a power-on that fails partway unwinds the domains
without running the park callback, which would otherwise leave a stale
cache to suppress the next programming.

Configuration is checked rather than trusted: the syscon and the table
are required together, thresholds must descend for the first-match lookup
to mean anything, the margin must fit the three-bit field, and every
enabled OPP has to land on an entry - an uncovered one would leave the
margin wherever the last transition put it.

